<!--ID=FCB40C2B-1F9F-4C26-B1A1-CF8E67BE07D1;Version=1|{"Files":{}}-->
<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.75%" pitch="-3.10%">
            He gives power to the faint.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            And to him who has no might, he increases strength.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            Even youths shall faint and be weary, and young men shall fall exhausted.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            But they who wait for the Lord shall renew their strength.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            They shall mount up with wings like eagles.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            They shall run and not be weary.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            They shall walk and not faint.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            Even the young grow tired.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Isaiah makes this point carefully. Youth is not protection against exhaustion. Strength is not permanent. Even the strong eventually reach their limit. Even the ones who seemed inexhaustible eventually sit down.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            And God gives power to the faint.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Not to the strong. Not as a reward for those who have kept themselves from growing tired. To the faint. To the ones who have run out. To the ones sitting in the dust, not sure they can stand again. That is who He turns toward. That is who receives the increase.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            They who wait for the Lord shall renew their strength.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            The word wait here is not passive. It carries the idea of binding together, of twisting strands into cord, of weaving. It is an active leaning toward God — not running ahead, not running away, but turning toward Him and staying there.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            And from that waiting, something rises.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Wings like eagles.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            Eagles do not beat their wings to rise. They spread them wide and find the thermal — the column of warm rising air — and they are carried up without effort. The rising is not their own doing. They yield to what is already lifting.
            <break time="3800ms"/>
            They shall run and not be weary.
            <break time="500ms"/>
            They shall walk and not faint.
            <break time="1800ms"/>
            This is where God is taking you.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            Wait on Him tonight.
            <break time="1000ms"/>
            Wings are coming.
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>
